    {
      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML certificate carefully.\n\nCertificate details:\n- Number: R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\n- Scheme: A\n- Project number: 2254799\n- Page total: 5\n- Member State: The Netherlands\n- Date issued: 14 August 2020\n- OIML issuer ID: NL1 (NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1)\n\nIssuing authority:\n- Name: NMi Certin B.V.\n- Address: Thijsseweg 11, 2629 JA Delft, The Netherlands\n- Person responsible: M. Boudewijns\n- Phone: +31 88 636 2332\n- Email: certin@nmi.nl\n- Website: www.nmi.nl\n- oiml_issuer_id: NL1\n\nApplicants:\n- ABB Limited, Oldends Lane, GL10 3TA Stonehouse, United Kingdom\n\nManufacturers:\n- Same as applicant: ABB Limited (Applicant and Manufacturer: ABB Limited)\n- Production locations also include ABB Engineering (Shanghai) Ltd. and ABB Inc. — these are production locations; the schema doesn't have production_locations explicitly. I could add them as manufacturers since they produce the meter. The markdown says \"The water meter is produced at one of the following production locations\". I'll include them as manufacturers entries, since they are production locations of the certified type. Hmm, but \"manufacturers[]\" — I think it's reasonable to include all three production locations as manufacturers, or keep the manufacturer as ABB Limited and note production locations. The schema only has manufacturers[]. I'll include all three as manufacturers with the role implied. Actually, let me be careful — \"Don't fabricate\". The applicant/manufacturer on page 1 is ABB Limited. Production locations are a different concept. But including them as manufacturers is a reasonable interpretation. I'll include all three in manufacturers[].\n\nCertified type:\n- category: \"Water meter\"\n- type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XX.R\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4*\"] — the type is \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XX.R and FET4 *\". Let me record: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XX.R\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4*\"]. The footnote explains XX: first X can be 1 or 3 (standard or enhanced), second X can be 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only).\n- module_designation: Not applicable? Not mentioned. Omit or null. I'll omit.\n- description: \"An electromagnetic water meter\"\n\nFootnote: the *) note should go into footnotes[] with marker \"*\".\n\nModel family: The cert covers a family of instruments. Models vary by:\n- Meter size: DN40, DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350 (11 sizes)\n- Power supply: AC mains powered vs Battery/Renewable energy powered\n- Accuracy class: 1, 2 (or 1 & 2)\n\nTable 2: Reduce bore - AC mains powered, flow rates per size and accuracy class.\nTable 3: Reduce bore - Battery / Renewable energy powered.\nTables 4 & 5: Indicating device characteristics per size for both power configs.\n\nSo the config axis here is power supply type. The tables are matrix tables. I should emit:\n- matrix_tables[] for Table 2, Table 3, Table 4, Table 5\n- model_family.models — how to define model_id? Could be per meter size + power config, e.g., \"DN40-AC\", or just sizes. Hmm. The family covers sizes × power supply. For model_level characteristics, flow rates vary per size and per power config and accuracy class. That's a 3-dimensional matrix (size × power × class). The schema's model_level is attribute × model. I could define models as combinations like \"DN40 AC mains class 1\" etc. That's a lot: Table 2 has 18 rows (some sizes have 2 class rows), Table 3 has 18 rows.\n\nLet me think about the best representation:\n\nOption A: matrix_tables hold the raw tables (4 tables), and model_family.models list sizes (DN40...DN350). model_level characteristics could include q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1 with values keyed by model = e.g. \"DN40 AC class 1\". That's messy.\n\nOption B: Use config_level for power supply axis, and model_level for size axis. But flow rates vary by both.\n\nI think the cleanest: \n- model_family.models: entries per meter size (11 sizes), with attributes like power_supply_options: [\"AC mains\", \"Battery/Renewable\"], accuracy_classes: [1, 2].\n- matrix_tables: 4 tables preserving the data.\n- characteristics.model_level: flow characteristics per model where model_id = \"DN40\" etc. But because Q1 etc. differ by accuracy class and power source, maybe better to key model_id by size+power+class? That would create ~36 models. Hmm.\n\nThe R137 example puts rows of the table as models keyed by size. Here we have two power-config tables plus class sub-rows.\n\nI think a reasonable approach: define models as \"DN40 (AC mains)\" etc. — but the class variation... The certificate says accuracy class 1 and 2. For sizes where both classes share the same Q values (e.g. DN65 class 1&2 in AC table: Q1=0.063), it's one row. For DN40, class 1: Q1=0.05, class 2: Q1=0.025.\n\nGiven the schema, I'll use model_level entries with models keyed as \"DN40-AC-C1\", \"DN40-AC-C2\", ... That's a lot of models but accurate. Alternatively keep matrix_tables as the primary structured representation and have model_level only for attributes that vary cleanly.\n\nActually, maybe simpler and defensible: model_family.models with model_id = meter size (e.g., \"DN40\") — since the type designation FEW4XX covers sizes. Then use matrix_tables to preserve full multi-dimensional data (size × class × power). And use config_level for power supply axis? The config_level is for attribute × config-axis matrix with conditions.\n\nHmm, the instructions say: \"Configuration axes. When attributes vary by configuration (not by model — e.g. 2-sensor-row vs 4-sensor-row setups; static vs dynamic weighing), use characteristics.config_level with axis describing the dimension.\"\n\nHere, power supply (AC mains vs battery) is a configuration axis; meter size is a model axis; accuracy class is another axis.\n\nBest structural approach given constraints:\n\n1. matrix_tables: 4 tables fully preserved (tables 2-5).\n2. model_family: models = 11 meter sizes, each with attributes {accuracy_classes: [1,2], power_supply: [\"AC mains\", \"Battery/Renewable energy\"]}.\n3. characteristics.type_level: general characteristics that don't vary (measuring principle, environmental class per component — that varies by component but it's textual; electromagnetic environment E1; water temperature class T50; MAP 1.6 MPa; orientation; flow profile sensitivity; reverse flow; software identification; pressure loss class varies by size though!).\n\nPressure loss class: Δp 40 (0.40 bar) for DN40 and DN50; Δp 65 (0.65 bar) for sizes larger than DN50. That varies by model (size). Could be a model_level attribute: pressure_loss_class: value \"Δp 40\" for DN40, DN50; \"Δp 65\" for others. Values are like \"Δp 40 (0,40 bar)\". I can structure: value: \"Δp40\", with additional numeric? Maybe attribute \"pressure_loss_class\" with value \"Δp 40\" and a companion \"pressure_loss\" value 0.4 bar. Let me do model_level entries:\n- pressure_loss_class: values DN40: \"Δp 40\", DN50: \"Δp 40\", DN65+ \"Δp 65\".\nMaybe include the parenthetical bar values in the value string: \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\". Strings are allowed for values.\n\nEnvironmental class varies by component: B for flow transmitter, O for flow sensor. That's component-level. I can put components: flow transmitter, flow sensor (implied by characteristics). Actually the characteristics table mentions \"flow transmitter\" and \"flow sensor\" — components of the meter. The type AquaMaster4 FEW4XX.R (integral?) and FET4* (remote?). Hmm, FEW = flanged electromagnetic watermeter? FET = flow meter transmitter? Not sure. I shouldn't fabricate. I could record components:\n- role: \"flow transmitter\", characteristics: environmental class B\n- role: \"flow sensor\", characteristics: environmental class O\n\nThe certificate text mentions these in Table 1 only. I'll add components with minimal info.\n\nTemperature range ambient varies by component too: +5/+55 °C for flow transmitter; -25/+55 °C for flow sensor. That's config_level or component-level. I could use config_level with axis \"component\": conditions \"flow transmitter\" / \"flow sensor\". That fits config_level nicely: attribute temperature_range_ambient, axis \"component\", values [{condition: \"flow transmitter\", value: {min:5,max:55}, unit °C}, {condition: \"flow sensor\", value: {min:-25,max:55}}].\n\nSimilarly environmental class: attribute environmental_class, axis component: B (installed in buildings) for flow transmitter; O (installed outdoors) for flow sensor.\n\nPower supply: three types listed:\n- AC mains: 85–240 VAC @ 50/60 Hz → power_supply_voltage {85–240 V AC}, power_supply_frequency {50–60 Hz}\n- Renewable energy power (DC powered): 6–32 V → power_supply_voltage_dc {6–32 V}\n- Replaceable battery: 3.6 V (operating range 2.9–3.6 V) → battery_voltage 3.6 V, battery_operating_range 2.9–3.6 V\n\nWater temperature class: T50 (+0.1 °C / +50 °C). Value \"T50\" with range note. I could add water_temperature_range {min: 0.1, max: 50} °C and water_temperature_class \"T50\". Hmm, \"+0,1 °C\" — the plus sign is odd; T50 class in R49 is 0.1–50 °C. I'll do water_temperature_class: \"T50\" and maybe water_temperature_range {min:0.1, max:50}.\n\nMAP: 1.6 MPa (16 bar): maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 MPa. Could also add 16 bar secondary. I'll do value 1.6 unit MPa, maybe another characteristic map_bar 16 bar? Keep simple: one entry with unit \"MPa\" and value 1.6; mention \"(16 bar)\" — could add note. I'll add second characteristic maximum_admissible_pressure_bar: 16 \"bar\". Hmm, that may be over-engineering. I'll include both since parenthetical is a value.\n\nFlow profile sensitivity class: U0 and D0 (0 × DN upstream and 0 × DN downstream). Value \"U0/D0\" string.\n\nReverse flow: \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\" → value boolean? String \"intended to measure reverse flow\". I'll use value: true? Better string.\n\nSoftware identification: version 03.00.03, checksum B26AFE6B → two characteristics: software_version \"03.00.03\", software_checksum \"B26AFE6B\".\n\nAccuracy class: \"1 and 2\" → in recommendation.accuracy_classes: [1, 2].\n\nNow model_level flow characteristics. Decision: I'll define models keyed by size+power+class? Or size+power? Let me count rows:\n\nTable 2 (AC mains):\n- DN40: C1 (Q1 0.05, Q2 0.08, Q3 25, Q4 31.25, ratio 500), C2 (0.025, 0.04, 25, 31.25, 1000)\n- DN50: C1 (0.08, 0.128, 40, 50, 500), C2 (0.04, 0.064, 40, 50, 1000)\n- DN65: C1&2 (0.063, 0.1008, 63, 78.75, 1000)\n- DN80: C1&2 (0.1, 0.16, 100, 125, 1000)\n- DN100: C1 (0.32, 0.512, 160, 200, 500), C2 (0.16, 0.256, 160, 200, 1000)\n- DN125: C1 (0.32, 0.512, 160, 200, 500), C2 (0.16, 0.256, 160, 200, 1000)\n- DN150: C1&2 (0.4, 0.64, 400, 500, 1000)\n- DN200: C1&2 (0.63, 1.008, 630, 787.5, 1000)\n- DN250: C1 (2, 3.2, 1000, 1250, 500), C2 (1, 1.6, 1000, 1250, 1000)\n- DN300: C1 (3.2, 5.12, 1600, 2000, 500), C2 (1.6, 2.56, 1600, 2000, 1000)\n- DN350: C1 (3.2, 5.12, 1600, 2000, 500), C2 (1.6, 2.56, 1600, 2000, 1000)\n\nTable 3 (Battery/Renewable):\n- DN40: C1&2 (0.0625, 0.1, 25, 31.25, 400)\n- DN50: C1&2 (0.1, 0.16, 40, 50, 400)\n- DN65: C1 (0.39375, 0.63, 63, 78.75, 160), C2 (0.1575, 0.252, 63, 78.75, 400)\n- DN80: C1 (0.625, 1, 100, 125, 160), C2 (0.25, 0.4, 100, 125, 400)\n- DN100: C1 (1, 1.6, 160, 200, 160), C2 (0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400)\n- DN125: C1 (1, 1.6, 160, 200, 160), C2 (0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400)\n- DN150: C1&2 (1, 1.6, 400, 500, 400)\n- DN200: C1&2 (1.575, 2.52, 630, 787.5, 400)\n- DN250: C1 (6.25, 10, 1000, 1250, 160), C2 (2.5, 4, 1000, 1250, 400)\n- DN300: C1 (10, 16, 1600, 2000, 160), C2 (4, 6.4, 1600, 2000, 400)\n- DN350: C1 (10, 16, 1600, 2000, 400), C2 (4, 6.4, 1600, 2000, 400)\n\nWait check Table 3 DN350 C2: ratio 400. Yes.\n\nTotal rows: 18 + 18 = 36 configurations.\n\nGiven the size, the cleanest is to preserve the tables in matrix_tables and generate model_level entries with model identifiers like \"DN40|AC mains|class 1\". That yields 36 models per attribute × 5 attributes (q1,q2,q3,q4,q3_q1_ratio) = 180 value entries. That's verbose but the schema asks for it. Alternatively, models could be keyed by size only, and the class/power variations captured via matrix_tables and config_level.\n\nLet me think about what's most useful and consistent with the rules. Rule 4 says detect matrix and populate model_family.models AND characteristics.model_level. The R137 example: models per size, attributes per model.\n\nHere the primary \"model\" axis is meter size. Power supply and accuracy class are configuration dimensions (the tables themselves are split by power config; class rows within). I could:\n\n- model_family.models: 11 sizes, model_id \"DN40\"...\"DN350\".\n- characteristics.model_level: attributes that vary by size alone: pressure_loss_class.\n- characteristics.config_level: two axes:\n  - axis \"power_supply\": attribute q1/q2/q3/q4/ratio... but these also vary by class and size. config_level values have {condition, value} — condition could be composite like \"DN40, AC mains, class 1\". That is essentially the same as model_level with different key name.\n\nHmm. Maybe better: define model_level where \"model\" = full configuration id like \"DN40 AC mains class 1\". The schema example for R60 used model_id \"Emax=25t\". So composite ids are fine.\n\nLet me define 36 models:\nAC mains:\n1. DN40 AC C1\n2. DN40 AC C2\n3. DN50 AC C1\n4. DN50 AC C2\n5. DN65 AC C1&C2\n6. DN80 AC C1&C2\n7. DN100 AC C1\n8. DN100 AC C2\n9. DN125 AC C1\n10. DN125 AC C2\n11. DN150 AC C1&C2\n12. DN200 AC C1&C2\n13. DN250 AC C1\n14. DN250 AC C2\n15. DN300 AC C1\n16. DN300 AC C2\n17. DN350 AC C1\n18. DN350 AC C2\n\nBattery:\n19. DN40 BAT C1&C2\n20. DN50 BAT C1&C2\n21. DN65 BAT C1\n22. DN65 BAT C2\n23. DN80 BAT C1\n24. DN80 BAT C2\n25. DN100 BAT C1\n26. DN100 BAT C2\n27. DN125 BAT C1\n28. DN125 BAT C2\n29. DN150 BAT C1&C2\n30. DN200 BAT C1&C2\n31. DN250 BAT C1\n32. DN250 BAT C2\n33. DN300 BAT C1\n34. DN300 BAT C2\n35. DN350 BAT C1\n36. DN350 BAT C2\n\nModel_id format: \"DN40-AC-C1\", \"DN40-AC-C2\", \"DN65-AC-C1C2\", \"DN40-BAT-C1C2\" etc. Attributes per model: accuracy_class, meter_size, power_supply_type, q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1.\n\nBut then model_level attribute entries: each attribute × 36 values. That's 8 attributes × 36 = 288 value objects. Very long JSON but fine? It might be ~2000 lines. Hmm, that's a lot of output. Alternatively, keep models at size level (11 models) and put flow rates in matrix_tables (4 tables, fully preserved) plus config_level for class axis.\n\nGiven the emphasis \"MOST IMPORTANT\" on matrix detection, and that matrix_tables preserve the raw multi-dim data, I think a balanced approach:\n\n- model_family.models: 11 sizes with attributes {accuracy_classes, power_supply_options}.\n- matrix_tables: 4 tables (tables 2, 3, 4, 5) fully preserved with rows.\n- characteristics.model_level: flow attributes (q1, q2, q3, q4, q3_q1_ratio) where model id encodes size+power+class. \n\nHmm, but that duplicates matrix_tables heavily. The R137 example did both: matrix_tables AND model_level. So duplication is expected by the schema.\n\nLet me reduce duplication cost: maybe model_level with models keyed \"DN40 AC mains class 1\" style. 5 flow attributes × 36 = 180 entries plus accuracy class attribute 36 = 216. Each value object ~4 tokens... it's maybe 3000-4000 tokens of JSON. Acceptable but large.\n\nAlternative: Use config_level for accuracy class axis: attribute q1, axis \"accuracy_class\", values [{condition: \"DN40 AC mains class 1\", value 0.05}...]. Same volume.\n\nAlternatively: model_level per size+power (22 combos), with values listing per class in... no, values must be single value per model.\n\nHmm. Actually, maybe simpler: since the class only affects Q1/Q2/ratio (Q3 and Q4 are same across classes for a size+power combo), I could:\n\n- model_level: q3, q4 keyed by size+power (22 models).\n- config_level: axis \"accuracy_class\" for q1, q2, ratio_q3_q1 with conditions like \"DN40, AC mains, class 1\".\n\nThat's cleaner semantically. But \"model\" in model_level then = \"DN40 AC mains\" composite. Hmm.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: maybe treat meter size as model axis and power supply as config axis, with accuracy class embedded in conditions. The schema config_level: {attribute, axis, values: [{condition, value}]}. Condition strings can be composite: \"DN40 / AC mains / class 1\". That works but hides structure in strings.\n\nI'll go with: \n- model_family.models: model_id per size (11): \"DN40\", ..., \"DN350\", attributes: {accuracy_classes: [...], power_supply_options: [...], pressure_loss_class: \"Δp 40\"/\"Δp 65\"}. Wait — model attributes in the example were empty {}. I can put attributes per model: meter_size, pressure_loss_class, accuracy_classes, power_supply_options. Good.\n- matrix_tables: 4 tables.\n- characteristics.model_level: flow attributes q1, q2, q3, q4, q3_q1_ratio with model ids that include size, power, class. Let me do this fully. Actually you know, let me just bite the bullet and do the full 36-model model_level. It's the most explicit and machine-usable. But the output length... Let me estimate: each attribute entry with 36 values: each value object like {\"model\": \"DN40-AC-C1\", \"value\": 0.05} ≈ 12 tokens. 36×12 = 432 tokens per attribute + overhead. 5 flow attributes + ratio + accuracy class... ~3000 tokens. Plus tables (36 rows × 7 fields) ~1500 tokens. Plus everything else. Total maybe 8-10k tokens output. That's fine.\n\nHmm wait, but do I include \"accuracy_class\" as model_level attribute? The models encode class in their id, making accuracy_class attribute redundant. Alternatively model ids exclude class, and accuracy_class is an attribute. But class varies within size+power. So model id must include class or class must be condition. I'll include class in model_id and not duplicate as attribute... Actually including accuracy_class as an attribute makes parsing easier for downstream consumers who filter attributes. But redundancy in JSON is fine.\n\nLet me decide the model_id format: \"DN40-AC-C1\", \"DN40-AC-C2\", \"DN65-AC-C1+2\", \"DN40-BAT-C1+2\". Use \"C1C2\"? I'll use \"class 1\", \"class 2\", \"classes 1&2\". Like \"DN40-AC-C1\", \"DN65-AC-C1C2\". Hmm, cleaner: \"DN40 AC mains class 1\". Spaces in ids are okay? The R60 example used \"Emax=25t\". I'll use hyphens: \"DN40-AC-C1\".\n\nFull list (AC):\n- DN40-AC-C1: Q1 0.05, Q2 0.08, Q3 25, Q4 31.25, R 500\n- DN40-AC-C2: 0.025, 0.04, 25, 31.25, 1000\n- DN50-AC-C1: 0.08, 0.128, 40, 50, 500\n- DN50-AC-C2: 0.04, 0.064, 40, 50, 1000\n- DN65-AC-C1C2: 0.063, 0.1008, 63, 78.75, 1000\n- DN80-AC-C1C2: 0.1, 0.16, 100, 125, 1000\n- DN100-AC-C1: 0.32, 0.512, 160, 200, 500\n- DN100-AC-C2: 0.16, 0.256, 160, 200, 1000\n- DN125-AC-C1: 0.32, 0.512, 160, 200, 500\n- DN125-AC-C2: 0.16, 0.256, 160, 200, 1000\n- DN150-AC-C1C2: 0.4, 0.64, 400, 500, 1000\n- DN200-AC-C1C2: 0.63, 1.008, 630, 787.5, 1000\n- DN250-AC-C1: 2, 3.2, 1000, 1250, 500\n- DN250-AC-C2: 1, 1.6, 1000, 1250, 1000\n- DN300-AC-C1: 3.2, 5.12, 1600, 2000, 500\n- DN300-AC-C2: 1.6, 2.56, 1600, 2000, 1000\n- DN350-AC-C1: 3.2, 5.12, 1600, 2000, 500\n- DN350-AC-C2: 1.6, 2.56, 1600, 2000, 1000\n\n(BAT):\n- DN40-BAT-C1C2: 0.0625, 0.1, 25, 31.25, 400\n- DN50-BAT-C1C2: 0.1, 0.16, 40, 50, 400\n- DN65-BAT-C1: 0.39375, 0.63, 63, 78.75, 160\n- DN65-BAT-C2: 0.1575, 0.252, 63, 78.75, 400\n- DN80-BAT-C1: 0.625, 1, 100, 125, 160\n- DN80-BAT-C2: 0.25, 0.4, 100, 125, 400\n- DN100-BAT-C1: 1, 1.6, 160, 200, 160\n- DN100-BAT-C2: 0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400\n- DN125-BAT-C1: 1, 1.6, 160, 200, 160\n- DN125-BAT-C2: 0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400\n- DN150-BAT-C1C2: 1, 1.6, 400, 500, 400\n- DN200-BAT-C1C2: 1.575, 2.52, 630, 787.5, 400\n- DN250-BAT-C1: 6.25, 10, 1000, 1250, 160\n- DN250-BAT-C2: 2.5, 4, 1000, 1250, 400\n- DN300-BAT-C1: 10, 16, 1600, 2000, 160\n- DN300-BAT-C2: 4, 6.4, 1600, 2000, 400\n- DN350-BAT-C1: 10, 16, 1600, 2000, 160\n- DN350-BAT-C2: 4, 6.4, 1600, 2000, 400\n\nThat's 36 models. model_family.models will list all 36 with attributes {meter_size, power_supply, accuracy_class}. And matrix_tables preserve tables 2 and 3 exactly. Then model_level: q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1 — 5 attributes × 36 values. Hmm, this is getting big but complete. Alternatively skip model_family listing 36 and just do model_level with those ids... The schema wants models[] populated. OK.\n\nActually wait — do I really want both the 36-model model_family AND 36-value × 5 attribute model_level AND the matrix tables? That's triple redundancy. The R137 example had 2 models so triple redundancy was cheap. For 36 models it's heavy. Let me reconsider.\n\nGiven the guidance \"preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere\" — tables 2 and 3 are 3-dimensional (size × power × class), which doesn't fit model_level cleanly... but actually it does fit if model_id is composite. The guidance says model_family + model_level is the primary representation, matrix_tables for tables that don't fit.\n\nDecision: I'll do:\n- model_family.models: 36 entries with attributes (meter_size, power_supply, accuracy_class).\n- characteristics.model_level: 5 flow attributes + ratio (36 values each).\n\nWait, ratio IS one of the 5 I listed (q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio). Yes 5 attributes.\n\n- characteristics.config_level: temperature range by component, environmental class by component? Those are components. I'll use components[] with characteristics instead? The schema: components[] have role, type_designations, alternatives, characteristics{}. I can nest: {\"role\": \"flow transmitter\", \"characteristics\": {\"environmental_class\": {...}, \"temperature_range_ambient\": {...}}}. And flow sensor similarly. That's cleaner than config_level. But components' characteristics shape isn't specified strictly; I'll use structured value objects.\n\nHmm, but the flow transmitter/flow sensor are only mentioned in Table 1 as context for environmental class and temperature. Are they \"named sub-assemblies\"? Yes — \"flow transmitter\" and \"flow sensor\" are named. I'll include them as components with those characteristics.\n\n- config_level: maybe not needed then. Or use config_level for power-supply-dependent voltage? No — those are separate characteristics (AC vs DC vs battery). Fine as type_level with distinct names:\n  - mains_power_supply_voltage: {85–240} V\n  - mains_power_supply_frequency: {50–60} Hz\n  - dc_power_supply_voltage: {6–32} V\n  - battery_voltage: 3.6 V\n  - battery_voltage_operating_range: {2.9–3.6} V\n\nPressure loss class varies by size → model_level with model = size? But my models are composite. Values per composite model: DN40-AC-C1 → \"Δp 40\"... that would multiply. Alternatively put pressure_loss in matrix? Or model_family.models[].attributes.pressure_loss_class — models have DN40 → \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\". Since all variants of a size share pressure loss class, putting it in model attributes keyed by size works if models were sizes... but my models are composites. Each of the ~3 composites per size would carry the same pressure_loss_class. That's fine — attributes per model entry.\n\nActually, let me reconsider model_family.models as 11 sizes with attributes, and model_level values keyed by composite ids that include size+power+class. Slight mismatch between models[] ids and model_level \"model\" keys. The R137 example had them match. Mismatch might confuse. \n\nAlternatively: model_level model key = size only, and accuracy class as config_level axis, power as... no, flow values differ by both power AND class.\n\nOK here's my final structure decision:\n\n- model_family.models: 36 composite entries. Each has attributes: {\"meter_size\": \"DN40\", \"power_supply\": \"AC mains\", \"accuracy_class\": 1}. For C1C2 models: accuracy_class: [1, 2].\n\n- characteristics.model_level: \n  - q1 (m³/h) — 36 values\n  - q2 (m³/h) — 36 values\n  - q3 (m³/h) — 36 values\n  - q4 (m³/h) — 36 values\n  - q3_q1_ratio (dimensionless) — 36 values\n  - pressure_loss_class (36 values, string) — hmm, this is 6×36=216 value objects. That's a LOT. Let me think about whether to include pressure_loss_class in model_level. Each composite model of same size shares it. 36 string values \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\". OK I'll include it — completeness matters. Actually, maybe simplify value to \"Δp 40\" with a note? The parenthetical gives bar value. I could add separate attribute pressure_loss (bar): 0.4 or 0.65. Let me do two model_level attributes: pressure_loss_class (string \"Δp 40\"/\"Δp 65\") and pressure_loss (bar, 0.4/0.65). Hmm, that's another 36. Or combine as string \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\". I'll do the combined string, one attribute.\n\n- Indicating device tables (4 & 5): per size, per power config. Attributes: indicating_range_max (9999999 m³ for all — could be type_level: 9999999 m³!), indicating_range_min varies by size group, verification_scale_interval_max varies by size group. These could go into matrix_tables (tables 4, 5) and optionally model_level with composite ids \"DN40-AC\", \"DN80-BAT\"... Different granularity from flow models (no class). Since indicating device doesn't vary by class, composite ids without class: \"DN40-AC\", \"DN50-AC\", \"DN40-BAT\" etc. But then model ids in model_level for these attributes (DN40-AC) don't match models[] ids (DN40-AC-C1). Hmm.\n\nOption: put tables 4 and 5 in matrix_tables only, and note indicating range max 9999999 m³ as type_level. And put indicating_range_min and verification_scale_interval_max in matrix tables only (they vary by size groups). That's acceptable — matrix_tables preserve them. I think that's reasonable to avoid a 22-entry × 2 attribute explosion. But the \"MOST IMPORTANT\" rule says varying attributes → model_level... The tables 4/5 are \"indicating device\" sub-component characteristics. I could treat the indicating device as a component and its characteristics in matrix_tables. \n\nHmm, let me weigh output size. 22 combos × 2 attributes = 44 more value objects. Not huge. But model id mismatch... I could use \"DN40-AC\" style and note these refer to indicating device configs. Actually you know what, matrix_tables for tables 4 and 5 fully capture them; downstream can join on size + power config. I'll do matrix_tables only for 4 & 5, plus maybe type_level indicating_range_max 9999999 m³. Hmm, but indicating_range_max is 9999999 in ALL rows of both tables — safe as type_level. I'll add it.\n\nAlso type_level: meter_size_range? Not stated. Skip.\n\nNow Table 1 general characteristics → type_level:\n- measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic\"\n- accuracy_class: \"1 and 2\" → string value \"1 and 2\"? Or omit since recommendation.accuracy_classes has [1,2]. I'll include accuracy_classes as string value... Rules: numbers not strings. \"1 and 2\" isn't a number. Could do value: [1,2]? Schema says value: number|{min,max}|string. Hmm. List isn't in the union. I'll use string \"1 and 2\". Or actually model attributes already carry class. I'll put type_level accuracy_class: \"1 and 2\". Fine.\n- environmental_class: component-dependent → components.\n- electromagnetic_environment: \"E1\"\n- temperature_range_ambient: component-dependent → components.\n- water_temperature_class: \"T50\" and water_temperature_range {0.1, 50} °C. Hmm the table says \"T50 (+0,1 °C / +50 °C)\". I'll do water_temperature_class value \"T50\" and water_temperature_range {min:0.1, max:50} unit °C.\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 MPa; plus maximum_admissible_pressure_secondary? \"(16 bar)\". I'll include maximum_admissible_pressure {1.6, MPa} and maximum_admissible_pressure_bar {16, bar}. Hmm — maybe one characteristic with unit MPa and mention bar in a second. OK two entries.\n- orientation: \"All positions (horizontal, vertical, or diagonal)\"\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0 and D0 (0 × DN upstream, 0 × DN downstream)\" → value string \"U0 and D0\". Maybe add zero_straight_pipe_lengths: \"0 × DN upstream and downstream\". I'll keep one string value.\n- reverse_flow: value string \"Sensor intended to measure reverse flow\" — or true. I'll use value: true, plus... hmm, semantics. Use string \"intended to measure reverse flow\". Eh. I'll do value: true with attribute reverse_flow_measurement_supported? The text: \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\". value: true. OK.\n- software_version: \"03.00.03\"\n- software_checksum: \"B26AFE6B\"\n- power supplies as above.\n- pressure_loss_class: model-dependent → model_level (by composite model) OR keep in type_level as conditional string? Rules say varying → model_level. I'll put in model_level.\n\nFootnote: marker \"*\" — \"The type designation XX can be on the first X for 1 or 3 (standard or enhanced) and the second X can be 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only.\" (note: text seems truncated \"sensor only.\" likely \"sensor only)\"). I'll reproduce as given, maybe fix to \"sensor only)\". I'll keep as in source.\n\nThe type designation footnote applies to \"FET4 *\". So type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XX.R\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4*\"] with footnote_markers [\"*\"] on the second? The footnote applies to the \"*\" in \"FET4 *\". I'll add footnote marker \"*\" to the FET4 designation... but type_designations is a list of strings; can't attach markers. I'll note it in the footnote and maybe in description. Fine.\n\nTest report: No. NMi-2254799-01 dated 14 August 2020, 235 pages, role: type_evaluation_report (it says \"OIML Type Evaluation Report\"). Yes role type_evaluation_report.\n\nDocumentation: \"Documentation folder no. T11851-1\" → test_reports[] entry with role \"documentation_file\", id \"T11851-1\", no date/pages. Schema allows id, date, pages, role — omit missing.\n\nRecommendation: id \"R49\", edition... \"R 49-1 (2013)\". Hmm — R49-1 is Part 1 of R49, 2013 edition. Canonical id: \"R49\". Edition: 2013. Amendment: null. The certificate says \"R 49-1 (2013)\". Should id be \"R49-1\"? The rule says canonical e.g. \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\". R49-1 is a part. I'll use id \"R49-1\" — hmm. The example canonicalizes \"OIML R 60\" → \"R60\". For multi-part, keeping the part seems right: \"R49-1\". I'll use \"R49-1\", edition: 2013 (as int? \"edition (int)\" → 2013). Wait edition (int) — 2013 is the year, used as edition for OIML. Yes, int 2013.\n\nscheme: \"A\".\naccuracy_classes: [1, 2].\n\nCertificate: number \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\", scheme \"A\", project_number \"2254799\", page_total 5, member_state \"The Netherlands\", date_issued \"2020-08-14\" or \"14 August 2020\"? I'll use ISO \"2020-08-14\". oiml_issuer_id \"NL1\".\n\nIssuing authority: name \"NMi Certin B.V.\", address_lines [\"Thijsseweg 11\", \"2629 JA Delft\", \"The Netherlands\"], person_responsible \"M. Boudewijns\", person_title \"Certification Board\" (appears near signature; the layout: \"Person responsible: M. Boudewijns\" then later \"Certification Board\" above NMi Certin B.V. block). The \"Certification Board\" label precedes NMi Certin B.V. — likely M. Boudewijns' title/department. I'll set person_title: \"Certification Board\". phone \"+31 88 636 2332\", email \"certin@nmi.nl\", website \"www.nmi.nl\", oiml_issuer_id \"NL1\".\n\nApplicants: ABB Limited, [\"Oldends Lane\", \"GL10 3TA Stonehouse\", \"United Kingdom\"].\n\nManufacturers: The cert says \"Applicant and Manufacturer: ABB Limited\". Production locations: three ABB entities. I'll include manufacturers: ABB Limited (same address). Should I add Shanghai and Warminster as manufacturers? They're production locations. The schema lacks production_locations. Adding them as manufacturers with their addresses is a reasonable, non-fabricated representation (they are listed as locations where the water meter is produced). I'll include all three. Hmm, risk of mislabeling? \"The water meter is produced at one of the following production locations\" — they produce it, so they are manufacturers. Include.\n\nComponents:\n- flow transmitter: environmental class B (installed in buildings), temperature range +5/+55 °C.\n- flow sensor: environmental class O (installed outdoors), temperature range -25/+55 °C, reverse flow measurement intended.\nNo type designations for components given (FEW/FET might map but don't fabricate; actually FEW4XX.R and FET4* are the type designations of the meter, not clearly transmitter/sensor). Leave type_designations omitted.\n\ncharacteristics.config_level: I decided components carry the env/temp. But schema also allows config_level; using components for this seems semantically right. Alternatively both. I'll use components' characteristics nested objects with structured values.\n\nWait — should components have \"characteristics\" in the layered shape? Schema: components[]: role, type_designations[], alternatives, characteristics{}. Yes.\n\nNested characteristics shape: I'll use the same structured-value convention: {\"environmental_class\": {\"value\": \"B\", ...}, \"temperature_range_ambient\": {\"value\": {\"min\":5,\"max\":55}, \"unit_symbol\":\"°C\"}}.\n\nNow matrix_tables:\n\nTable 2: name \"General characteristics of the family — Reduce bore, AC mains powered\"\ncolumns: [\"meter_size\", \"accuracy_class\", \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\", \"q3_q1_ratio\"]\ncolumn_units: [null, null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null]\nrows: 18 rows as object per row.\n\nTable 3: same columns, battery/renewable powered. 18 rows.\n\nTable 4: name \"Indicating device characteristics — Reduce bore, AC mains powered\"\ncolumns: [\"meter_size\", \"indicating_range_max\", \"indicating_range_min\", \"verification_scale_interval_max\"]\ncolumn_units: [\"mm\", \"m³\", \"m³\", \"m³\"]\nrows:\n- {\"meter_size\": \"DN40, DN50, DN65\", ...} Hmm meter size groups. Keep as strings: \"DN40, DN50, DN65\". Or split into separate rows per size with same values? Splitting duplicates but is cleaner for joins. I'll keep groups as strings to preserve fidelity, or split? The R137 example used one size per row. Here groups. I'll keep group strings — \"Don't fabricate\" splitting is fine though (it's faithful: each of DN40, DN50, DN65 has these values). I'll split for machine-usability: rows for DN40, DN50, DN65 each with same values. Hmm, that inflates rows from 5 to 11 per table. It's fine.\n\nActually wait — verification scale interval max for AC: DN40/50/65: 0.0001; DN80: 0.0001 (but min 999999); DN100-200: 0.001; DN250: 0.001 (min 9999999); DN300/350: 0.01.\n\nAC table rows split:\n- DN40: 9999999, 99999, 0.0001\n- DN50: 9999999, 99999, 0.0001\n- DN65: 9999999, 99999, 0.0001\n- DN80: 9999999, 999999, 0.0001\n- DN100: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\n- DN125: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\n- DN150: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\n- DN200: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\n- DN250: 9999999, 9999999, 0.001\n- DN300: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\n- DN350: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\n\nBAT table:\n- DN40: 9999999, 99999, 0.0001\n- DN50: 9999999, 99999, 0.0001\n- DN65: 9999999, 99999, 0.001\n- DN80: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\n- DN100: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\n- DN125: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\n- DN150: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\n- DN200: 9999999, 999999, 0.01\n- DN250: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\n- DN300: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\n- DN350: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\n\nDecimal conversions: 0,0001 → 0.0001; 0,001 → 0.001; 0,01 → 0.01.\n\nNow model_level flow values — write them carefully.\n\nq1 (unit m³/h):\nAC: DN40-C1 0.05; DN40-C2 0.025; DN50-C1 0.08; DN50-C2 0.04; DN65-C1C2 0.063; DN80-C1C2 0.1; DN100-C1 0.32; DN100-C2 0.16; DN125-C1 0.32; DN125-C2 0.16; DN150-C1C2 0.4; DN200-C1C2 0.63; DN250-C1 2; DN250-C2 1; DN300-C1 3.2; DN300-C2 1.6; DN350-C1 3.2; DN350-C2 1.6.\nBAT: DN40 0.0625; DN50 0.1; DN65-C1 0.39375; DN65-C2 0.1575; DN80-C1 0.625; DN80-C2 0.25; DN100-C1 1; DN100-C2 0.4; DN125-C1 1; DN125-C2 0.4; DN150 1; DN200 1.575; DN250-C1 6.25; DN250-C2 2.5; DN300-C1 10; DN300-C2 4; DN350-C1 10; DN350-C2 4.\n\nq2:\nAC: 0.08, 0.04, 0.128, 0.064, 0.1008, 0.16, 0.512, 0.256, 0.512, 0.256, 0.64, 1.008, 3.2, 1.6, 5.12, 2.56, 5.12, 2.56.\nBAT: 0.1, 0.16, 0.63, 0.252, 1, 0.4, 1.6, 0.64, 1.6, 0.64, 1.6, 2.52, 10, 4, 16, 6.4, 16, 6.4.\n\nq3:\nAC: 25, 25, 40, 40, 63, 100, 160, 160, 160, 160, 400, 630, 1000, 1000, 1600, 1600, 1600, 1600.\nBAT: 25, 40, 63, 63, 100, 100, 160, 160, 160, 160, 400, 630, 1000, 1000, 1600, 1600, 1600, 1600.\n\nq4:\nAC: 31.25 ×2 (DN40), 50 ×2 (DN50), 78.75 (DN65), 125 (DN80), 200×4 (DN100, DN125), 500 (DN150), 787.5 (DN200), 1250×2 (DN250), 2000×4 (DN300, DN350).\nBAT: 31.25 (DN40), 50 (DN50), 78.75×2 (DN65), 125×2 (DN80), 200×4 (DN100,125), 500 (DN150), 787.5 (DN200), 1250×2 (DN250), 2000×4 (DN300,350).\n\nratio_q3_q1:\nAC: 500, 1000, 500, 1000, 1000, 1000, 500, 1000, 500, 1000, 1000, 1000, 500, 1000, 500, 1000, 500, 1000.\nBAT: 400, 400, 160, 400, 160, 400, 160, 400, 160, 400, 400, 400, 160, 400, 160, 400, 160, 400.\n\nNote: the certificate says flow rates \"can be freely chosen as long as...\" — Q1/Q2 are minimum values, Q3/Q4 maximum values, with Q2/Q1=1.6, Q4/Q3=1.25. Should I record this as constraints? Could add type_level notes or matrix footnotes. I'll add these as... maybe a \"notes\" in matrix table description. The matrix_tables schema has description and footnotes. I'll put the constraint text into each flow table's description. Good.\n\nActually also Q2/Q1 = 1.6 exactly: check DN40 C1: 0.08/0.05 = 1.6 ✓. DN65 AC: 0.1008/0.063 = 1.6 ✓.\n\nmodel_id naming: I'll use like \"DN40-AC-C1\", \"DN65-AC-C1C2\", \"DN40-BAT-C1C2\". And in model_family.models give attributes: {\"meter_size\": \"DN40\", \"power_supply\": \"AC mains\", \"accuracy_class\": \"1\"} or [1,2]. Hmm accuracy_class value type: number for single, list for both. Schema attributes{} is free-form. OK.\n\nActually, should model_family.models include indicating device attrs and pressure loss? Keep attributes minimal: meter_size, power_supply, accuracy_class(es). Pressure loss per size goes in model_level attribute... but model_level model keys are composite. pressure_loss_class for each composite: DN40-* → \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\" (4 composites), DN50-* (4) → same, others → \"Δp 65 (0.65 bar)\". 36 entries for pressure_loss_class. Hmm. That's another 36-value attribute. I'll include it — yes, for completeness.\n\nActually, let me reconsider — 6 attributes × 36 values = 216 objects. Each object like {\"model\": \"DN40-AC-C1\", \"value\": 0.05} — compact one-line. JSON lines fine.\n\nHmm, one more thought: should I really include 36 model entries in model_family.models AND matrix tables AND model_level? Yes — following the R137 pattern (models + matrix_tables + model_level all populated).\n\nAlternatively, to reduce, skip matrix_tables for tables 2/3 since model_level covers them? But rule 4 example shows both. And tables 4/5 only in matrix_tables. I'll keep all four tables in matrix_tables.\n\nNow type_level list final:\n- measuring_principle: {value: \"Electromagnetic\"}\n- accuracy_class: {value: \"1 and 2\"} — hmm. Or omit? I'll include as string.\n- electromagnetic_environment: {value: \"E1\"}\n- water_temperature_class: {value: \"T50\"}\n- water_temperature_range: {value: {min: 0.1, max: 50}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: {value: 1.6, unit_symbol: \"MPa\"}\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 16 bar as separate: attribute \"maximum_admissible_pressure\" only once... I'll name second \"maximum_admissible_pressure\" {value 1.6, \"MPa\"} and add \"maximum_admissible_pressure_bar\"? That's clunky. Alternative: value 1.6 unit MPa; note (16 bar) via footnote? No footnote exists. I'll add second attribute \"maximum_admissible_pressure\" with... can't duplicate keys in JSON. Use \"maximum_admissible_pressure_bar\": {value: 16, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}. Acceptable.\n\nHmm, actually maybe cleaner: maximum_admissible_pressure: {value: 1.6, unit_symbol: \"MPa\"} and skip the bar. The parenthetical conversion is useful though. Keep both.\n\n- orientation: {value: \"All positions (horizontal, vertical or diagonal)\"} — string.\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: {value: \"U0 and D0\"} plus straight_pipe_lengths: \"0 × DN upstream / 0 × DN downstream\"? I'll combine: value \"U0 and D0 (0 × DN upstream and 0 × DN downstream)\". Fine as string.\n- reverse_flow: {value: true}? or string. I'll do value: true with attribute reverse_flow. Hmm \"reverse_flow\": true conveys support. OK.\n- mains_power_supply_voltage: {min:85, max:240}, \"V\"\n- mains_power_supply_frequency: {min:50, max:60}, \"Hz\"\n- mains_power_supply_type: \"AC\"\n- dc_power_supply_voltage: {min:6, max:32}, \"V\" (renewable energy / DC powered)\n- battery_voltage: 3.6, \"V\", replaceable battery\n- battery_voltage_operating_range: {min:2.9, max:3.6}, \"V\"\n- software_version: \"03.00.03\" — keep leading zeros as string ✓\n- software_checksum: \"B26AFE6B\"\n- indicating_range_max: 9999999, \"m³\" (from tables 4/5, constant)\n- bore_type: \"Reduced bore\"? Tables say \"Reduce bore\". All tables are reduce bore only. Could add meter_bore: \"Reduced bore\". Hmm, the word \"Reduce bore\" in table headers suggests family limited to reduce bore. I'll add attribute \"bore_type\": \"Reduced bore\". Actually source says \"Reduce bore\" — I'll use \"Reduced bore\" normalized... to avoid fabrication, use \"Reduce bore\"? I'll write \"Reduced bore\" as it's clearly the meaning. Hmm, minor. I'll use value \"Reduced bore\".\n\nmodel_level attributes:\n- q1 m³/h (36)\n- q2 m³/h (36)\n- q3 m³/h (36)\n- q4 m³/h (36)\n- q3_q1_ratio (36)\n- pressure_loss_class (36) — strings \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\" / \"Δp 65 (0.65 bar)\". Hmm Δp character — fine, it's not LaTeX.\n\nWait, also should indicating device attrs go model_level with \"DN40-AC\" style ids? I decided matrix_tables only. But consistency... eh. Actually, let me add them too with model ids \"DN40-AC\", ... that's 22 ids × 2 attributes = 44. It gives the \"MOST IMPORTANT\" rule full treatment. Hmm, but those ids aren't in models[]. I could instead add indicating attrs per composite model (36): indicating_range_min and verification_scale_interval_max per composite (max range is type_level). DN